---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MachineTop from '../components/MachineTop.astro';
import SampleList from '../components/SampleList.astro';
import { bySlug } from '../data/machines';

const m = bySlug('ko-mono-20');

// The strip under the header: the page's sections, the one being read underlined.
const sections = [
  { id: 'download', label: 'Download' },
  { id: 'hear', label: 'Hear it' },
  { id: 'accuracy', label: 'In numbers' },
  { id: 'differences', label: 'Differences' },
  { id: 'made', label: 'How it was made' },
  { id: 'artwork', label: 'Artwork' },
];
---
<Base title="ko-mono-20 · Lemus" description="ko-mono-20 emulates the Korg MS-20, the 1978 Rev 1 with its KORG35 filters and the later Rev 2, modelled part by part from the service manual and checked against recordings of real units. Free for macOS and Windows." current="ko-mono-20">

<nav slot="sub" class="onpage" aria-label="On this page">
<div class="frame sheet">
<div class="col">
<ol>
{sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`} class:list={{ red: s.id === 'differences' }}>{s.label}</a></li>)}
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</nav>

<MachineTop machine={m}>
<SampleList heading="Bass, lead and the scream" plugin="ko-mono-20" tracks={[
  { src: '/media/ko-mono-20/bass.m4a', title: 'Bass', detail: "VCO 1's saw at 16', EG 2 on the LPF" },
  { src: '/media/ko-mono-20/lead.m4a', title: 'Lead', detail: "Both VCOs at 8', vibrato from the MG" },
  { src: '/media/ko-mono-20/scream.m4a', title: 'The scream, Rev 1', detail: 'LPF PEAK 9.5, CUTOFF swept up and down' },
  { src: '/media/ko-mono-20/scream-rev2.m4a', title: 'The scream, Rev 2', detail: 'The same, on the KLM-307 filter board' },
]} />
<Fragment slot="data">
<div><dt class="lbl">Filter boards</dt><dd>Rev 1 (KORG35) and Rev 2 (KLM-307), in SERVICE</dd></div>
<div><dt class="lbl">Patch panel</dt><dd>35 jacks, with your DAW's audio into ESP or EXT SIGNAL IN</dd></div>
</Fragment>
</MachineTop>

<section class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<figure class="panel">
<div class="mount">
<div class="pin">
<img src="/media/ko-mono-20/panel.png" alt="ko-mono-20: the MS-20's panel with its knobs, the patch panel with no cables in it and the ESP, the keyboard with the control wheel and the momentary switch, the KORG logo's place left blank, and the plugin bar with SERVICE and ?" width="2732" height="1426">
<span class="leader" style="left: 34.4%; top: -24px; height: calc(12.3% + 24px)" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="target" style="left: 34.4%; top: 12.3%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="balloon" style="left: 34.4%; top: -56px" aria-hidden="true">A1</span>
<span class="leader" style="left: 69.4%; top: -24px; height: calc(13.6% + 24px)" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="target" style="left: 69.4%; top: 13.6%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="balloon" style="left: 69.4%; top: -56px" aria-hidden="true">A2</span>
<span class="leader" style="left: 3.7%; top: 97.2%; height: calc(2.8% + 24px)" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="target" style="left: 3.7%; top: 97.2%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="balloon" style="left: 3.7%; top: calc(100% + 24px)" aria-hidden="true">A3</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="caption">
<div class="row"><span class="key">A1</span><span><strong>The filters.</strong> The HPF and the LPF are two KORG35 modules, as the service manual draws them, and run as circuits with the rest of the voice.</span></div>
<div class="row"><span class="key">A2</span><span><strong>The patch panel.</strong> Every jack takes cables as on the unit, and a click on EXT SIGNAL IN or ESP SIGNAL IN plugs in your DAW's audio.</span></div>
<div class="row"><span class="key">A3</span><span><strong>SERVICE.</strong> CIRCUIT swaps the filter board for the later Rev 2's KLM-307.</span></div>
<div class="row"><span class="fig">FIG. 1</span><span>No cables, the KORG logo's place left blank, and the plugin bar with SERVICE and ?.</span></div>
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>

<section id="accuracy" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<div class="numbers-head">
<h2 class="h2 tap">In numbers</h2>
<p class="dim">There's no MS-20 here, so we checked ours against the service manual's own adjustment procedure and against free recordings of about 20 real units. Method: <a href="#made">How it was made</a>.</p>
<p class="dim small">The clips at the top are ko-mono-20 alone, with no effects, playing lines we wrote. The two screams are the same patch on each filter board.</p>
</div>

<div class="numbers">
<div class="measure">
<div class="figure">
<div class="big">All 46</div>
<div class="what">of the service manual's adjustment checks pass</div>
<div class="dim small">The Rev 2's filter trim is one of them. Every block's netlist also matches ngspice running the same circuit within 1 %, in 78 cases.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="measure">
<div class="figure">
<div class="big">2 dB</div>
<div class="what">band by band between the Rev 2's scream and a real one</div>
<div class="dim small">That's the one recording we have of a Rev 2, played next to a Rev 1, and we fitted the Rev 2's diodes and trims to it. On the Rev 1, a stock unit screaming its filter up sounds very much the same as ours on the same patch.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="measure">
<div class="figure">
<div class="big">26 %</div>
<div class="what">of one CPU core at 48 kHz, holding a note</div>
<div class="dim small">That's both its threads together, on an M-series Mac. Everything at once with both filters self-oscillating takes about 50 %, and once it has gone quiet the circuit sleeps and its output is exact zeros.</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="differences" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<h2 class="h2 tap red">What is still different</h2>
<dl class="diffs">
<div><dt>Self-oscillation over the VCOs</dt><dd>With PEAK up and a VCO playing, our LPF's whistle locks onto a harmonic and wobbles with vibrato, where one real unit's runs free between the harmonics. Another unit locks on as ours does, though, so it may just be that one.</dd></div>
<div><dt>A cleaner whistle</dt><dd>A real self-oscillating filter has a skirt of noise around its tone. Ours comes out cleaner, because we don't model the parts' own noise in the filters.</dd></div>
<div><dt>The start of a scream</dt><dd>While CUTOFF is low, a screaming LPF sweep starts 2-4 dB brighter than a real one above 2 kHz. Ours also needs PEAK 9.9 to match one unit at 9.5.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Values against the drawings</dt><dd>We use 33k for R161 where the drawing prints 1K, because one unit's envelope sweeps all want that, and 4.3k for R169 where the printed 43K would cap SUSTAIN at 13 %. Real boards have an R62 100K that the diagrams leave out, so ours has it too. On the Rev 2, R38 is 4.7k (printed 3.3K) and the VCA runs on about a fifth of the drawn current, so it comes out as loud as the Rev 1.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Ranges and levels</dt><dd>CUTOFF covers about 11.5 octaves as the drawing has it, where the specification says 50 Hz to 5 or 15 kHz, and the knob readings in recordings agree with the drawing. SIGNAL OUT reaches 0.95 Vpp where the block diagram says 2, and EG 1 rests at -7.5 V where the specification says -5.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Noise</dt><dd>The noise jacks give 2.1 Vpp against the specification's 5, and there's no 1/f or burst noise.</dd></div>
</dl>
<div class="not">
<h3 class="h3">Not emulated</h3>
<p class="dim">We don't model the power supply's ripple, the unit warming up or part tolerances, so every ko-mono-20 is the same unit (each one drifts and hisses on its own, though). PHONES isn't a plugin output, so you hear SIGNAL OUT.</p>
</div>
<div id="help" class="help">
<div class="help-text">
<h3 class="h3">Own an MS-20?</h3>
<p>A clean recording of the LPF self-oscillating up the keyboard, with both VCOs turned down, would settle the whistle. Dry recordings of a Rev 2 with its settings written down would check what we fitted to one recording, and a photo of the main board where R161 and R169 can be read would settle two values we had to guess.</p>
</div>
<a href="https://lemus.discourse.group" class="btn red">The forum</a>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="made" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<h2 class="h2 tap">How it was made</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li>
<div class="step-n">01</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">The circuit</h3>
<p class="dim">We transcribed every block from the service manual's circuit diagrams, part by part, and checked each netlist against ngspice running the same circuit. The VCOs, the filters and the VCA run as circuits at four times your DAW's rate, and the envelopes, the MG and the rest of the control side run on a second thread.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-n">02</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">The service manual</h3>
<p class="dim">Then we trimmed it with the manual's own adjustment procedure, the way a technician would, and all 46 of its checks pass.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-n">03</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">Real units</h3>
<p class="dim">We compared ours with free recordings of about 20 real MS-20s, event by event: the oscillators' waveforms and levels, the filters' sweeps and resonance, the FM depth, the MG's rates, the noise and the envelopes. Where the drawings leave a value out or get it wrong, we fitted it to those recordings (see <a href="#differences">Differences</a>).</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-n">04</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">The Rev 2</h3>
<p class="dim">We modelled the later KLM-307 filter board from its own drawing. Its limiter diodes, two resonance trims and VCA drive aren't printed or have no procedure, so we fitted them to the one recording we have of a Rev 2 played next to a Rev 1.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>

<section id="artwork" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec brief last">
<h2 class="h2 tap">Panel artwork</h2>
<p class="dim">KORG's logo is a trademark, so its place on the panel, bottom right, is blank. It takes an SVG or PNG file of your own, named <span class="mono">ko-mono-20-maker</span>, in the ROM folder. Draw it yourself or generate it with an AI tool such as Claude or ChatGPT. <a href="/ko-mono-20/guide#artwork">Panel artwork</a></p>
<p class="dim">ko-mono-20 is a circuit with no firmware, so it needs no ROM.</p>
</div>
</section>

</Base>

<script>
// Underline the strip's link for the section being read: the last whose top has passed under the
// strip, or the last of all at the foot of the page.
const links = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.onpage a')];
const targets = links.map((a) => document.getElementById(a.hash.slice(1))!);
const strip = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.onpage ol')!;
let current = -2;
function mark() {
  let i = -1;
  targets.forEach((t, j) => { if (t.getBoundingClientRect().top <= 80) i = j; });
  if (innerHeight + scrollY >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 2) i = targets.length - 1;
  if (i === current) return;
  current = i;
  links.forEach((a, j) => { if (j === i) a.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location'); else a.removeAttribute('aria-current'); });
  // Bring the link into view along the strip only. scrollIntoView would also scroll the page, up
  // by the scroll padding, since the stuck strip sits inside it.
  const a = links[i];
  if (!a) return;
  const r = a.getBoundingClientRect(), s = strip.getBoundingClientRect();
  if (r.left < s.left) strip.scrollLeft += r.left - s.left;
  else if (r.right > s.right) strip.scrollLeft += r.right - s.right;
}
let frame = 0;
const later = () => { if (!frame) frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => { frame = 0; mark(); }); };
addEventListener('scroll', later, { passive: true });
addEventListener('resize', later);
mark();
</script>

<style>
